lesson g5-rates-and-timetables v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Rates, and the 24-hour timetable"
  goal "Compare speeds, prices and crowding by working out how much for one, and use a speed to plan a trip by the 24-hour clock."

  look {
    scene 36x14 {
      departureboard board platforms=0 mark=-1 head=["stop", "leaves"] trains=["Mill, 13:40", "Ash Bridge, 14:05", "Hill Top, 14:35", "Quarry, 15:20"] at=canvas(1, 1)
      doubleline line steps=4 topTo=24 bottomTo=4 topUnit="km" bottomUnit=hours mark=1 right-of=board gap=1
    }
    say """
      A rate is how much for one: the boat goes 24 km in 4 hours, which is 6 km in each hour. Two
      speeds, two prices or two crowded places are compared by finding how much for one of each, or
      how much for the same amount of each. On the canal the boats keep 24-hour time, so after
      midday the hours carry on from 12: twenty to two in the afternoon is 13:40.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice rates.canoe-minute count=1 seed=3
    level easy {
      worked rates.boat-speed v=4 h=2 t=2.5 level=medium
    }
    practice rates.boat-speed count=1 easy-count=2 seed=5
    practice rates.faster-boat count=1 seed=4 hard-count=2
    practice time.trip-length count=1 seed=4
    practice rates.better-buy count=1 seed=2
    practice time.latest-boat count=1 seed=6 easy-count=0
    practice rates.busy-moorings count=1 seed=7
    practice rates.village-density count=1 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show rates.speed-mistake v=5 k=7 easy-v=4 easy-k=5 hard-v=6
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show rates.first-to-arrive s=1 easy-s=0 hard-s=2
  }
  remember {
    say "To compare two rates, find how much for one of each. After midday, add 12 to the hour."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The question to ask every time is how much for one: one minute, one hour, one roll, one square
    kilometre. More kilometres is not the same as faster when the trip also took longer, more boats
    is not the same as more crowded when one bank is longer, and a bigger bag can cost less for each
    roll. A child who is stuck comparing two speeds can also scale both trips to the same number of
    hours. For times, count minutes on to the next o'clock and then on again; a child who subtracts
    13:40 from 15:20 as ordinary numbers gets 180, not 100 minutes. In the two-star question the
    child picks what Nell did wrong: she added the same number to both rows of the double number
    line, and a rate multiplies. The three-star question gives no method: each boat's arrival time
    has to be found from its speed and its start, and the faster boat does not always win. If a
    rate is still found by adding, stay here another day. Off the page, time a walk to the shop and
    work out the metres in a minute.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the canoe goes for four or five minutes at 60 or 80 m a minute, 4 km an hour for two and a half hours is worked once in the teacher's pen before trips of one and a half hours asked twice, the faster boat is the canoe, the trip takes 80 minutes, the bags have 4 and 8 rolls, there is no latest boat to choose, the busier bank also has more boats, the villages divide easily, Nell's mistake is at 4 km an hour, and in the three-star boat B leaves 25 minutes later and arrives first."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the canoe goes for six or eight minutes at 45 or 75 m a minute, the boats go at 5 or 6 km an hour for two and a half or three and a half hours, which boat is faster is asked twice and the one that went further is, the trip takes 115 minutes, the deadline is ten or twenty past with a longer trip, the bags have 6 and 10 rolls, the busier bank has fewer boats, the villages' crowding is close, Nell's mistake is at 6 km an hour, and the three-star is 18 km with a trip of four and a half hours and starts more than two hours apart."
  }
}
